  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 217 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nOn Friday, the upper low will be moving into western ID while the\nhigh shifts into the ARKLATEX region. This will cause a subtle\nveering in the winds aloft, introducing drier air into far western\nNM while pushing the best moisture/instability axis over the\ninterior high terrain of the state. PWATs would likely reduce a\ntenth of an inch or two in central areas, but would still hover\naround 1.0 inch or slightly more. This would support fewer, but \nstill numerous efficient rain-producing storms with a slight \nreduction in flash flooding potential Friday afternoon. The NAM is\na bit of an outlier with less QPF and lower spatial coverage of\nstorms on Friday, so it will be interesting to see which solutions\nget favored with tonight's CAM runs. Storms would be more \ninclined to move into the plains, given the veering steering flow,\nbut may not make it all the way to the OK and TX borders. \n\nMore dry air spills into NM from the west on Saturday, as the\naforementioned upper low shifts toward western WY. While the\nincoming dry air could reduce the overall heavy rainfall threat \nin western and central NM, the dry-moist demarcation that will \nprecede the trough axis (extending into the Four Corners) could \nprove to be a focal point for strong storm development. Deeper \nmoisture holding on in the south central mountains and east \ncentral plains will also fuel scattered storms there Saturday \nafternoon.\n\nThe remnants of the low will shift farther east into the northern\nGreat Plains and weaken on Sunday. This will steer a more due\nwesterly wind component aloft into NM with continued drying. Not\nsurprisingly, PWATs lower enough to suppress most storm\ndevelopmentand blended guidance has finally started to reduce \nadvertised POPs. The caveat to this will be a backdoor front that \nmay just barely reach Union county in northeastern NM. Models have\nbacked off on the invasion of this front for Sunday, so low \n20-30% POPs have been retained in the far northeast to account for\nthis. The front will enter more of northeastern NM Sunday night, \nbut appears to largely wash out by Monday with surface winds \nveering mostly south southeasterly over the plains. However, by \nthis time a new Pacific low is projected to take shape west of NM.\nThis low could begin to draw a moist east southeasterly return \nflow into NM Monday night with this moisture getting synced up \nwith another monsoon plume to our south. This would lead to a more\nactive storm period Tuesday into Wednesday of next week with \nanother significant uptick in rainfall and perhaps strong to \nsevere storms.",
